/** @mainpage

This suite exercises tsf-shell - terminal-emulator sessions from the
Test Agent it runs on - against a terminal-emulator backend
(libghostty-vt, or libvterm), on deterministic output the suite produces
on the agent itself:

- @ref shell-render - a shell prints a known screen with escape
  sequences; the rendered text is read back and asserted row by row;
- @ref shell-cursor - a cursor-positioning sequence puts the cursor
  where the test said, and tsf-shell reports that cell;
- @ref shell-attrs - SGR colour and attribute sequences produce cells
  whose palette index, foreground/background and bold/underline/reverse
  flags are what was set;
- @ref shell-expect - an interactive shell is driven: input is typed in,
  the echo is waited for on the output stream (classic expect) and the
  same text is found on the rendered screen with its cell coordinates;
- @ref shell-resize - resizing the session reaches the child (its
  @c stty @c size changes) and the emulator's own screen size follows;
- @ref shell-wait - a spawned program's exit code and signal are
  reported back;
- @ref shell-conformance - a fixed escape script is fed in and the whole
  rendered grid is compared to a golden expectation - a programmatic
  vttest, with the emulator as the thing under test.

There is no mock and no network: the emulator is a library linked into
the agent's RPC server and driven in-process, so every byte is rendered
locally and deterministically. A backend is required; a test skips
(`tapi_shell_available()`) when none is linked in. Numeric results
(characters painted per row, colour channels per cell, round-trip
latency, screen size across a resize, exit codes, cells matching the
golden) are emitted as MI measurement artifacts, so the HTML log and
Bublik draw them as graphs. The point of the suite is that tsf-shell
drives a real TTY and reads a real terminal screen back as structured
data.

*/
